{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791031825,"nonce":"QZ6e+hAViSjusfkTLtcTmZ9WcIU=","title":"Wayland FIFO: frames queued ahead of the compositor stall for a second","message":"On the NUC (GNOME 50.1 headless, Mesa 26.0.3 lavapipe, ashui Fifo) a continuously animating window draws 3 frames then the next surface acquire waits out wgpu's 1 s timeout, repeating: about 3 fps.\n\nProtocol trace: Mesa's Wayland WSI sends each present with wp_commit_timer_v1.set_timestamp and wp_fifo_v1 set_barrier/wait_barrier. ashui's loop, while animating, waits 1/120 s and draws, so it presents every ~11 ms, faster than the 16.7 ms refresh; target timestamps run ahead of what was presented, and after a presented event mutter holds the two queued commits for a full second without presenting either.\n\nEvidence it is the queueing: with the loop slowed below the refresh (wait 1/50), 0 acquire timeouts and steady 23 ms gaps. Mailbox has no stalls but no pacing (~90 fps from the loop's own wait). FifoRelaxed and Immediate are not offered by this surface.\n\nFix: draw on the compositor's frame callback, as Blinc does (Blinc git-bug e17d13a): call winit's pre_present_notify before each present, and when animating requestRedraw and draw on RedrawRequested instead of waiting a fixed 1/120 s. Needs Window.prePresentNotify in hlwindow; asked xwindow-f0. ashui already reconfigures the surface after an acquire timeout (Blinc's second half).\n\nWhether a real display stalls the same way is not yet known: over ssh there is no GPU, so this was measured in a headless session.","files":null}]}